WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.733
Question 0011: a random final tic-tac-toe board,
a senior-level question.

2
00:00:04.733 --> 00:00:09.200
Two parts: the regular board,
and diagonals that wrap around to the other side.

3
00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:15.233
Generate a board where a game has ended,
together with its result: X, O or a draw.

4
00:00:16.033 --> 00:00:21.233
Part A: what are all the conditions of a final board,
and how do we write them as constraints?

5
00:00:21.933 --> 00:00:27.433
The cells are numbered 0 to 8, row by row.
X always starts, and the players alternate.

6
00:00:28.233 --> 00:00:33.333
This is the box from the page, with the question's board:
X has two lines, and X won.

7
00:00:33.333 --> 00:00:38.633
Condition 1, the count: X starts and the players alternate.
Three X and no O never happens in a game.

8
00:00:38.633 --> 00:00:43.667
Condition 2: there are no two winners.
The game stops the moment someone completes a line.

9
00:00:43.667 --> 00:00:48.800
The condition most candidates forget: X has a line,
but n_X = n_O. So O moved after the win.

10
00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:53.767
Without the last O, n_X = n_O + 1.
The winner is the one who moved last.

11
00:00:53.767 --> 00:00:58.800
The same for O: a line of O,
no line of X, and n_X = n_O.

12
00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:03.100
And a draw: a full board, five X and four O,
with no line at all.

13
00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:09.433
Four conditions: the count, and one per result.
Each one will become a constraint.

14
00:01:10.233 --> 00:01:14.533
Now let's write the class in SystemVerilog,
piece by piece.

15
00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:27.500
Three types: a cell is EMPTY, X or O,
and the result is one of three.

16
00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:31.300
The board is an array of 9 cells,
numbered 0 to 8.

17
00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:37.100
The class ttt_end. Every call to randomize()
gives a final board and a matching result.

18
00:01:49.467 --> 00:01:53.967
The table of lines: rows, columns and two diagonals.
The last four are kept for part B.

19
00:01:55.400 --> 00:01:59.900
n_lines = 8 on the regular board. It is not rand,
so the solver treats it as a constant.

20
00:02:03.233 --> 00:02:07.400
The board and the result are rand:
the solver picks both together.

21
00:02:14.933 --> 00:02:19.433
And helper variables, rand as well:
how many X and O, and which lines are complete.

22
00:02:29.933 --> 00:02:34.433
c_count: the helpers equal the count on the board,
and n_x is n_o or n_o + 1.

23
00:02:53.367 --> 00:02:57.867
c_lines: x_line[l] is 1 exactly when the line is active
and all three of its cells are X.

24
00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:09.400
x_lines and o_lines count the complete lines.
The int' cast widens the sum beyond one bit.

25
00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:30.100
c_result: each result implies its own conditions,
exactly the conditions from the box.

26
00:03:30.100 --> 00:03:34.100
An implication, not an equality: the cases are disjoint,
so every board determines one result.

27
00:03:37.333 --> 00:03:41.833
solve result before board: first a result,
with equal probability, and only then a board.

28
00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:48.000
And the class closes. The page also has show(),
which only prints the board, so it is left out.

29
00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:54.467
In c_count: three X and zero O.
No n_o satisfies that, so the board is rejected.

30
00:03:54.467 --> 00:03:59.567
In c_lines: the question's board has two complete X lines,
so x_lines = 2.

31
00:03:59.567 --> 00:04:04.867
X_WINS requires n_x == n_o + 1.
Here n_x == n_o: O moved after the win.

32
00:04:04.867 --> 00:04:10.033
O_WINS: a line of O, none of X,
and n_x == n_o, because O moved last.

33
00:04:10.033 --> 00:04:16.000
DRAW: a full board with no lines. Without n_x + n_o == 9
a board in mid-game would also be a draw.

34
00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:21.100
With solve before, the solver picks the result first.
This draw came out X_WINS.

35
00:04:21.100 --> 00:04:23.600
The next draw came out O_WINS.

36
00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:26.767
And the third: a draw,
which now comes out a third of the time.

37
00:04:27.567 --> 00:04:33.167
958 final boards: 626 for X, 316 for O, 16 draws.
Without solve before, every board is equally likely.

38
00:04:33.167 --> 00:04:38.767
Then X wins 65% of the draws, and a draw only 1.7%.
With solve before, each result gets a third.

39
00:04:38.767 --> 00:04:42.767
The same 958 boards, with a different distribution:
the rare case, the draw, gets tested far more.

40
00:04:43.567 --> 00:04:48.800
The solution space is small: 3 to the power of 9 fillings,
and only 958 of them are final boards.

41
00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:55.100
A trap: requiring exactly one line for the winner.
Here X closed two lines in one move, at cell 0.

42
00:04:55.100 --> 00:04:59.100
A constraint that is too strong hides legal boards,
and no test will complain about it.

43
00:04:59.883 --> 00:05:05.050
Part B: a diagonal continues on the other side of the board,
as if the board were glued to itself.

44
00:05:05.750 --> 00:05:10.983
For example cells 0, 5 and 7:
go down-left, leave the board and come back on the right.

45
00:05:11.783 --> 00:05:17.783
Four new lines, 12 lines in all:
four directions, each with three parallel lines.

46
00:05:17.783 --> 00:05:21.783
The conditions do not change, only the set of lines.
In the class it is one variable: n_lines.

47
00:05:22.583 --> 00:05:25.583
This is how the class is used with the 12 lines.

48
00:05:26.916 --> 00:05:29.883
Create an object of the class from part A.

49
00:05:31.250 --> 00:05:35.750
n_lines = 12 turns on the four new lines.
Not one line of the constraints changes.

50
00:05:39.683 --> 00:05:43.350
A regular draw: X or O,
each in half of the draws.

51
00:05:49.450 --> 00:05:53.950
randomize() with adds a temporary constraint.
Here it always fails, because there is no draw.

52
00:05:55.450 --> 00:05:59.350
In the box: a full board with no line,
a draw with the 8 lines.

53
00:05:59.350 --> 00:06:04.316
Turn on the wrap-around diagonals:
now both X and O have a line.

54
00:06:05.116 --> 00:06:09.116
A draw needs a full board: five X with no line.
Let's show that every 5 cells contain a line.

55
00:06:09.116 --> 00:06:14.616
Assume no line. Each direction splits the cells 2, 2, 1,
so it has 2 pairs on a line.

56
00:06:14.616 --> 00:06:19.083
Over the four directions: 8 pairs.
But 5 cells have 10 pairs.

57
00:06:19.083 --> 00:06:24.316
And every pair of cells lies on exactly one line.
A contradiction: no 5 cells without a line.

58
00:06:25.116 --> 00:06:30.583
In the box: a square of 4 cells with no line.
The stars: every empty cell completes a line.

59
00:06:30.583 --> 00:06:35.550
Cell 8 completes two lines: the diagonal 0, 4, 8,
and 1, 3, 8, which wraps around.

60
00:06:35.550 --> 00:06:38.683
And cell 2 completes the top row.

61
00:06:39.483 --> 00:06:44.716
With the 12 lines there are 1,314 final boards:
882 for X, 432 for O, and no draw.

62
00:06:44.716 --> 00:06:48.716
The solver finds no solution with DRAW:
it proves the theorem for us.

63
00:06:49.514 --> 00:06:55.114
To sum up: four conditions, each one a constraint.
The winner is the one who moved last.

64
00:06:55.114 --> 00:07:00.014
And with the wrap-around diagonals there is no draw.
Try it in the box on the page of question 0011.
